; Phalanx - the flying fortress (GAMEPLAY-DESIGN A7, roster type EIGHT).
;
; THE ESCALATION ANCHOR. P4.6 says a pressured enemy fields heavier things rather
; than more things, and until this landed `HEAVY_TYPES` was [aegis, screamer] -
; a bomber that correctly leaves defensive garrisons (A.q1) and a jammer with no
; weapon. So "the war escalates" meant "the war gets foggier" and nothing else.
;
; THESE NUMBERS ARE THE SECOND DRAFT, and the first flight is why. The user built
; it in their head as a capital ship and got a gunboat: "i imagined the Phalanx
; way more massive, like a battle ship... its turrets are too small to
; individually take down, i find the Phalanx to die before any of its turrets die
; individually. its should be like a flying fortress."
;
; The mount-stripping arc was never being SEEN, and the reason was arithmetic
; rather than logic. At 140 hull and 3 mounts of 30, the whole body was 230 points
; against a blaster that lands about 275 a second - the entire fight, guns and
; all, was over inside a second. Nothing was broken; there was simply no time for
; a sequence to read as a sequence.
;
;   hull 140 -> 700, mount_hull 30 -> 100, mount_count 3 -> 6.
;
; Six mounts of 100 sit in front of a 700 hull, so there are 1300 points to work
; through and the guns come off one at a time, on the bearings you choose, while
; the volume of return fire visibly falls. The scene grew to match - a 6 x 2 x 8 m
; hull with a tower and a keel, guns out on the beam at 3.4 m - because "too small
; to individually take down" was as much about SIZE as about hull.
;
; THE SCREEN IS NOW TWO ROTATING SHELLS, NOT A TRACKING ARC (A.q10). The arc
; worked and could not be READ - the user reported it as a bug twice and the
; second report was measured and correct - so they replaced the mechanic: "move
; away from a dynamically directional shield and do another option you've
; suggested - a rotating shield that needs good timing to penetrate... rotating
; around the ship in some patterns that can confuse the attacker."
;
;   shield_arc_deg 250, shield_slew_deg_s 28   DELETED
;   shield_outer_gaps 3, gap 48 deg, rate +26 deg/s
;   shield_inner_gaps 2, gap 76 deg, rate -16 deg/s
;   shield_max 300 -> 0
;
; THE POOL WENT TO ZERO ON PURPOSE, and it is the least obvious change here. The
; arc SPENT shield points on every covered hit, so a blaster emptied 300 in about
; a second and the mechanic simply stopped happening for the rest of the fight -
; half of why it never read. The rotating screen REFUSES a covered round instead:
; nothing is spent, it never falls, and the counter is timing rather than
; attrition. That makes this a barrier where the aegis is a battery, which is
; also the cleanest statement yet of "two shielded types, different axes".
;
; THE PAIR WAS MEASURED, not guessed - the same discipline the arc/slew pair got,
; and it refuted the first design on the first run. A pilot at 30 m, 90 s per row,
; averaged over 8 start phases:
;
;   pilot                          open%   median window   worst dry spell
;   camped on one bearing           17%        1.58 s          9.2 s
;   fast thoughtless orbit 47 deg/s 17%        0.87 s          5.0 s
;   flying WITH the outer shell     42%        1.81 s          2.5 s
;   flying WITH the inner shell     40%        1.15 s          1.7 s
;   ONE shell only (the control)    38%        1.50 s          2.5 s
;
; Read the first and third rows together: reading the pattern and flying with it
; is worth 2.5x the damage of sitting in one slot, and that is P4.2's anti-orbit
; job carried by a mechanism the player can SEE rather than by a shield that
; chased them. Read the last row on its own: a SINGLE rotating shell hands a
; camper 38% - it is barely a shield at all - which is why "some patterns that
; can confuse the attacker" had to be built deliberately rather than hoped for.
;
; The rates are the window-length knob and the gap widths are the fraction knob,
; and they are close to independent: camp% is the product of the two duty cycles,
; while window length is a gap width divided by the RELATIVE rate. Tune the one
; that matches the complaint.
;
; THE STERN VENT is the third skill and the only one that is aim: arc 26 deg,
; open 2.0 s in an 11.0 s cycle (18%). The plate is a real 1.6 x 1.2 m body, so
; splash cannot exploit it and a steady gun can. The cycle is deliberately not a
; neat ratio of either shell's period (13.8 s and 22.5 s per revolution), so the
; three rhythms never collapse into one number to memorise.
;
; damage 5 x fire_rate 0.8 x 6 mounts = 24 damage a second at full strength,
; falling as you strip it. It is deliberately close to the first draft's output
; despite doubling the gun count: the fight is now three times longer, and holding
; the old damage rate over the new duration would have made the type a wall rather
; than a siege.
;
; speed 5 / accel 4 / turn_speed_deg 55 - it is a capital ship. `turn_speed_deg`
; is the MOUNT slew here, not the body's, which is what a jinking pilot degrades.
;
; strength_cost 10 makes it far and away the most expensive unit in the manifest -
; a node buys ONE of these instead of ten raiders. That is P4.6's escalation
; expressed as a price rather than as a spawn rule.
;
; NOTHING HERE IS BALANCED. Per H6 these are authored starting points; the harness
; measures the result and the human's hands decide the feel.
;
; ONE THING LAYER 1 CANNOT SEE, stated so nobody quotes it wrong: `Lethality`
; knows this body's hull and its battery of mounts, and knows NOTHING about the
; rotating screen - there is no pool left for it to price, and a barrier's cost
; is a duty cycle rather than a number of points. So Layer 1 now reports the time
; to chew through 1300 points of metal with every round landing, which is the
; BEST case for the player and the floor of the real fight. Multiply it by the
; open fraction above for the honest range: about 2.4x for a pilot who reads the
; pattern, about 6x for one who camps. That gap IS the mechanic, and it is the
; instrument's output rather than an error.

